.TH PING 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
ping \- send ICMP echo requests to a host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ping
.RB [ \-c
.IR count ]
.I host
.SH DESCRIPTION
Resolve
.I host
and send it
.I count
ICMPv4 echo requests (default 4), one per second, printing the
round\-trip time for each reply and a summary (packets sent/received,
loss percentage, min/avg/max round\-trip time) at the end.
.P
Tries an unprivileged Linux "ping socket" first
.RB ( SOCK_DGRAM
+
.BR IPPROTO_ICMP ,
gated by the
.B net.ipv4.ping_group_range
sysctl) and only falls back to a raw socket -- which needs root or
.B CAP_NET_RAW
-- if that's refused. On a system with a permissive
.B ping_group_range
this often needs no special privilege at all.
.SH NOTES
IPv4 only -- ICMPv6 is a different protocol number with a different
checksum rule, not implemented yet. No
.BR \-i / \-W
to change the interval or per\-reply timeout (fixed at 1 second each
for now). No signal handler for an early Ctrl\-C summary -- the fixed
default count exists partly so a plain
.B "ping host"
can't hang forever by default.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR, and \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one reply was received, 1 if none were, 2 on a usage
error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7)
